Here's the recommended approach using `Window` lifecycle events and `Preferences`:

**1. Create a custom `Window` subclass:**

```csharp
public class AppWindow : Window
{
    public AppWindow(Page page) : base(page) { }

    protected override void OnStopped()
    {
        base.OnStopped();
        if (Page is AppShell shell)
        {
            var vm = shell.CurrentPage?.BindingContext as NotesViewModel;
            if (vm != null)
                Preferences.Set("draft_text", vm.DraftText);
        }
    }

    protected override void OnResumed()
    {
        base.OnResumed();
        if (Page is AppShell shell)
        {
            var vm = shell.CurrentPage?.BindingContext as NotesViewModel;
            if (vm != null)
                vm.DraftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
        }
    }

    protected override void OnDestroying()
    {
        base.OnDestroying();
        // Android back-button can skip Stopped entirely
        if (Page is AppShell shell)
        {
            var vm = shell.CurrentPage?.BindingContext as NotesViewModel;
            if (vm != null)
                Preferences.Set("draft_text", vm.DraftText);
        }
    }
}
```

**2. Return it from `App.CreateWindow`:**

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
        => new AppWindow(new AppShell());
}
```

**3. Your ViewModel just needs the bindable property:**

```csharp
public partial class NotesViewModel : ObservableObject
{
    [ObservableProperty]
    private string _draftText = string.Empty;
}
```

### Key things to know

| Point | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| Save in **`OnStopped`** | Fires when the app is fully backgrounded |
| Also save in **`OnDestroying`** | Android's back button can skip `Stopped` |
| **Don't** save in `OnDeactivated` | That fires for dialogs/split-screen — too noisy |
| `Resumed` **doesn't** fire on first launch | Initial sequence is `Created` → `Activated` |
| Keep handlers **fast** (<2s) | Avoids ANR on Android / watchdog kills on iOS |

`Preferences` is ideal here since draft text is small. For larger state, serialize to a file instead.
